 today  ideology is a better framework to  explain court decisions  however understanding the ideology of  justices  isn't perfectly predictive either let me  talk a bit about what we know about that  during the civil rights era from about  the 1950s to the 1980s  the supreme court played an active and  critical role  in advancing civil rights and civil  liberties for a number of  sub-populations  in the u.s many have argued that without  the influence of several  key supreme court decisions the civil  rights movement itself  would not have been as successful as it  was  but beginning in 1980 things began to  change  america had 12 years of ideologically  conservative presidents  with ronald reagan and george h.w bush  and together they made six appointments  to the supreme court  it was during this era that a clear  ideological pattern  began to emerge on the nine-member court  where a block of four conservative  justices voted together  a block of four liberal justices voted  together  and a swing justice was pivotal in many  cases  for example in the court's 2000  term justice william rehnquist a nixon  appointee  who was elevated to chief justice by  reagan  and conservative clarence thomas a bush  appointee  voted together in 99 of all cases in  fact  rehnquist thomas antonin scalia  and anthony kennedy often voted together  as a block  around the turn of the century to boot  the court's most  liberal justices stephen breyer  ruth bader ginsburg david suter and john  paul stevens  also generally voted together for many  years  justice sandra day o'connor a moderate  conservative  appointed by ronald reagan in 1981 and  the first woman to serve on the supreme  court  she was the swing justice  in 2000 the court heard a critical case  bush versus gore that helped to settle  legal uncertainties and irregularities  arising from the presidential election  that year  in that case o'connor sided with the  conservatives in a 5-4  vote that handed the presidency to  george w bush  all told in that term about one-third of  cases  were decided by five to four votes  when justice o'connor retired in 2006  anthony kennedy became the new swing  justice  shifting the median vote of the court a  bit to the right  justice kennedy retired in 2018  and today's supreme court has a solid  block  of five conservatives and four liberals  looking at the court just prior to  kennedy's retirement  in its 2017 term there were many five to  four decisions  and they all favored the conservative  lien on the court  the court's 2018 term looked different  but perhaps not in the way you might  expect if  ideology explains everything looking at  the five to four decisions in the 2018  term half of those favored the  conservatives  and half favor the liberals generally  speaking  in fact the court set a record in its  2019  term for having the most variety in the  various blocks that formed over the  course of that term  so both liberals and conservatives on  the court  have defected from the traditional  alignments that we observed so  strongly at the end of the 20th century  this means that in recent years there  really is  no swing justice on the court at least  not in the way that o'connor and kennedy  played for so long  all told the majority of decisions that  the court  hands down now are falling in a  conservative direction  given the balance of ideology on the  court but  the justices that make up the  conservative majority  on any given case are not always the  block that one might expect  ideology is therefore a useful way  to understand the supreme court and its  decisions but  it's far from the most predictive at  least when looking at the current court  when thinking about the role of the  supreme court throughout u.s history and  its politics  i broadly think about three distinct  periods  the period from 1789 until the civil war  in the 1860s  then the period between the civil war  through world war ii  then the period after world war ii until  now  in that first period the major conflicts  that the supreme court got involved with  were  those involving federalism the court was  petitioned to hear many fewer cases back  then compared to today  but those it heard often dealt with  settling whether  states or national government had power  over something  for example the court considered cases  like mcculloch versus maryland  that directly dealt with federal power  over state power  all in all the court was not asked to  intervene in matters  very often during the first 70 years or  so of u.s history  and when it did it was on issues of  grand importance and scale  during this period the court generally  sided with the federal government  over state governments and many  historians  see this as an effort of institutional  preservation  in that era the persistence of the  united states as a viable government  was somewhat uncertain  the country was new and the powers  established for the government  were bold yet vulnerable as a key  institutional feature of the new  american experiment in democracy  the supreme court tended to decide  questions in favor of the side  that lent more legitimacy to the  authority of the federal government to  exist  for many of these early years john  marshall was the chief justice of the  court  when roger b tawney became chief justice  in 1836  the court shifted more toward states  rights  which exacerbated tensions that  contributed to the american civil war  from 1861 to 1865.  in addition in 1857 the tawny court  decided  dred scott versus sanford in which the  court sided with a slave owner  when a missouri slave tried to sue for  his freedom  the case galvanized support for slavery  lending it legitimacy  that directly contributed to the civil  war  during the second period from the middle  of the 1800s through world war  ii most of the conflicts that the court  was asked to resolve  had to do with government versus the  economy  both of which greatly expanded during  that period  the industrial revolution was a period  filled with great innovation and  technological advancements  in transportation communication  agriculture  electricity and more these developments  fundamentally changed the way most  people in america  experienced their everyday lives goods  people and services could move more  easily around the country  and the rapid expansion of tools and  technology  allowed many more people to share in an  expanding  prosperity during this period  america also experienced intense growth  in the population  driven by mass immigration and advances  in medical care  all of these changes significantly  increased demands on government  the physical space of the u.s also  greatly expanded  as the country achieved its so-called  manifest  destiny by acquiring all of the  continental land from ocean to ocean  over a great latitude people needed more  from government  during this time and governments at all  levels  rapidly grew to meet these needs when  disputes were brought  to the court they were often about  whether to prioritize  the expanse of industry or the  government's ability to regulate  industry  at the beginning part of this period  courts tended to side with  industry as society's elites strongly  favored the robust  economic expansion that was propelling  many people into positions of privilege  but as this went on it became  increasingly clear that not everyone was  benefiting from this expansion  in fact those who were benefiting in  many cases were doing so  at the expense of others particularly  when it came to workplace labor abuses  and harmful working conditions  by the beginning of the 20th century  calls to protect the disadvantaged had  become louder  and the court eventually began to swing  towards government's ability  to regulate the economy over industry's  right to grow  after world war ii the court entered a  critical period  of resolving disputes between  individuals and government  and it largely remains in this general  paradigm today  beginning with the court's early action  on landmark civil rights cases  like brown versus board of education and  civil liberties cases like  miranda versus arizona the court  intervened in critical questions in the  20th century  concerning the role of government and  establishing and protecting liberties  and equalities for individuals  it is no accident that the period of  history that included the greatest  expansion of government  was followed by a period in which courts  were asked  to settle disputes about the appropriate  roles of government  today the court is confronted with  disputes  related to abortion gun rights  immigration laws and access to health  care  despite being asked to hear many cases  today's court only takes on less than  one percent of those requests  this is primarily a workload constraint  because each supreme court case involves  dozens of filings  briefs multiple parties and often  complicated questions  the nine justices often go through many  negotiations and drafts on a single case  and despite having clerks and other  forms of assistance  it's difficult to imagine the court  increasing its workload  beyond its current capacity  every once in a while some politician or  activist gets interested in  making reforms to the supreme court  while  it is often considered the most revered  respected and widely approved of  america's  federal government institutions it's  also  the most mysterious the supreme court  has never allowed  cameras in its oral testimonies supreme  court  justices themselves are often quite  elusive  rarely making public appearances the  supreme court  does allow audio recordings during oral  testimony  but the overall lack of access and  minimalist media provide the supreme  court with a sort of  veil of secrecy some have suggested  that it's the distance that the court  maintains from the public  that helps to contribute to its sense of  authority legitimacy and reputation as  an  impartial arbiter however in modern  american politics where  it seems like everything is hyper  partisan and polarized  it's becoming increasingly difficult for  the supreme court  to maintain this distance and reputation  it may be worthwhile to remember that a  little less than a hundred years ago  there was a rather serious effort to  reform the supreme court  it was during the great depression and  frank president franklin delano  roosevelt  was interested increasing the role of  the federal government  in regulating the economy as a way of  lifting the united states into recovery  however the court was not cooperative at  first  between 1935 and 1937  roosevelt was met with steady resistance  as the supreme court denied and rejected  many of his attempts at government  expansion and economic regulation  in part due to his frustration roosevelt  began promoting the possibility of  expanding the court  to include not nine but 15 justices  one additional justice for every justice  currently older than 70 and a half  he suggested that by adding six  additional justices to the court  that he himself would select it would  tip the balance of power  in the court in his favor roosevelt's  proposal became known  as the court packing plan  there is no question that a president  manipulating the supreme  court in the way that roosevelt proposed  is  a rather dramatic violation of  democratic norms  as the only president in u.s history to  be elected four times to the presidency  roosevelt was not particularly loyal to  the norms of democracy  but in the end his court-packing plan  didn't materialize  because two justices decided to side  with roosevelt and uphold the  constitutionality  of the social security act and the  national labor relations board  two key provisions of roosevelt's new  deal policy  with the court on his side roosevelt let  the court packing plan go  the change of heart by those justices  became known as  the switch in time that saved nine  meaning that  the permanence of the nine justice court  was preserved because  those justices had changed their minds  there have not been serious attempts at  reforming the supreme court since then  and personally i would not take very  seriously  any major reforms you may have heard  about  the institutions of the u.s government  are stable  and by design are very difficult to  change  like all of the political institutions  in the u.s the supreme court  has its flaws and its strengths yet  despite these  it has done a pretty good job at  maintaining its legitimacy  and reputation  [Music]  you
 

